WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.668
-Our next guest is
a supermodel, actress,

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:04.503
"New York Times" best-selling
author you know from films

00:00:04.503 --> 00:00:06.505
such as "Gone Girl"
and "I Feel Pretty."

00:00:06.505 --> 00:00:10.142
New episodes of her podcast
"High Low with EmRata" drop

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:12.979
every Tuesday and Thursday
across all podcast platforms.

00:00:12.979 --> 00:00:15.815
Please welcome back to the show
Emily Ratajkowski, everyone.

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:17.583
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:23.856 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.856 --> 00:00:30.096 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:31.931
Welcome back.
-Thank you.

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.699
-I am so happy to have you here.

00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:34.734
Last time you were here,

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:36.369
we talked a little bit about
your son.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:37.870
He's coming up on 2 years.
-Yep.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.072
-We just had Halloween. Did you
have an enjoyable Halloween?

00:00:40.072 --> 00:00:41.541
-I did.

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:43.309
It was the first Halloween he
really got what was going on.

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:45.378
Last year he was like,
"Ah, whatever."

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:47.547
So I dressed him up with my dog.

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:50.482
-I really appreciate a dog and
kid costume that has this --

00:00:50.482 --> 00:00:51.917
-Okay, thank you so much.

00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:53.753
-That just ties together
like this.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.988
-It honestly -- It came together
very quickly.

00:00:55.988 --> 00:00:57.357
-Yeah.
[ Audience aws ]

00:00:57.357 --> 00:01:02.261
That is just an excellent,
excellent Little Red Riding Hood

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:03.262
and Big Bad Wolf situation
right there.

00:01:03.262 --> 00:01:04.530
-Thank you so much.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.200
My son was not convinced of
the cape until he saw --

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.503
His first word is dog, but he
says "doggin" for every dog.

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:14.373
So when he saw "doggin" in the
hat, he was, like, ready to go.

00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:16.309
-Gotcha.
-And then we hit the streets.

00:01:16.309 --> 00:01:19.145
-And now, who got more attention
on the streets?

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.814
The baby or the dog?
-So we were on a stoop in the

00:01:21.814 --> 00:01:25.051
West Village with four babies,
like all adorably dressed.

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:29.689
And this man was sitting at the
top of the steps, and it was

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:31.724
like I wasn't there,
the babies weren't there,

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:34.127
it was just all about Columbo.

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:36.496
-I think if you can get a dog
in a bonnet,

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:37.897
people have a real appreciation.

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:39.532
-He had an emotional journey,
though.

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:40.600
-Yeah. Oh, interesting.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.868
-First he felt proud.
He was loving it.

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:44.536
Then he had shame.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.536 --> 00:01:47.072
Like, the bonnet was --
We had a whole thing.

00:01:47.072 --> 00:01:48.207
We were there for an hour,

00:01:48.207 --> 00:01:50.009
and people were like zooming in
on him.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:52.145
It was amazing.
-I like the idea that you think

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:54.714
there was a moment where your
dog transferred into shame.

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:56.482
-I will show you the pictures.
-"What am I doing?"

00:01:56.482 --> 00:01:58.117
-No, he was like,
"I'm really into it.

00:01:58.117 --> 00:01:59.452
I'm really into it."

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:01.154
He understood, like,
"I'm the big brother.

00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:02.455
I have to be committed to this."

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:04.623
Like, "it's putting our little
baby in the mood."

00:02:04.623 --> 00:02:05.892
I swear to God.

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:08.461
I don't know if y'all have dogs,
but like it's real.

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:11.164
And then all of a sudden, he
just laid down and was like...

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:12.532
[ Audience aws ]

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:14.734
...and had a little existential
crisis, but it's okay.

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:17.570
We made it through, and we won't
do it again, you know.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:18.837
-And still, you got
a perfect picture,

00:02:18.837 --> 00:02:20.439
and that's all that matters.
-Thank you.

00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:23.409
-Have you seen your son develop
a sense of humor?

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:24.510
Do you feel like that's --
-Yes.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:25.778
-This is around the age
that happens.

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:29.649
-Yes. He loves to be,
like, thrown around.

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:31.016
-Okay.
-That sounds questionable.

00:02:31.016 --> 00:02:32.551
-So he's like
a physical comedian.

00:02:32.551 --> 00:02:35.487
-Yes, he's very physical.
Definitely loves when I make

00:02:35.487 --> 00:02:37.456
funny faces, put on glasses,
all of that.

00:02:37.456 --> 00:02:40.192
But, like, he loves to just
get thrown on the bed.

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:42.829
And then, if you put a pillow
on him, he just giggles,

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:44.196
like laughs underneath
the pillow

00:02:44.196 --> 00:02:46.031
and just wants you to drop it
on him multiple times.

00:02:46.031 --> 00:02:47.767
-Yeah.
-So I do it.

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:48.935
-Yeah.
-I oblige.

00:02:48.935 --> 00:02:50.402
-Look, you're a people pleaser,
you know.

00:02:50.402 --> 00:02:51.937
-Yeah. Yeah, yeah.
-If he finds something he likes,

00:02:51.937 --> 00:02:54.106
you got to stick with it.
-No, and also, making your kid

00:02:54.106 --> 00:02:55.341
laugh, there's nothing better.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:56.642
-It's the best thing
in the world.

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:58.811
Nothing's more heartbreaking
than how much funnier

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:00.680
my kids think my wife is
than me.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.047
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.047 --> 00:03:05.318
Breathtaking, 'cause
in real life, it ain't close.

00:03:05.318 --> 00:03:07.086
[ Laughter ]
-I don't know.

00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:08.821
Are you sure?
-I'm not sure anymore.

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:10.022
-I mean, I dunno,
maybe your wife --

00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:12.324
-I have a lot of self-doubt.
-Yeah.

00:03:12.324 --> 00:03:14.660
-I want to ask -- We talked --
You did --

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:17.797
You wrote a book of essays.
Now you have a podcast.

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:19.965
Did writing the essays
in any way

00:03:19.965 --> 00:03:22.201
inform the choice to do
a podcast?

00:03:22.201 --> 00:03:24.203
Did you always think it was
something you wanted to do?

00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:27.206
-Totally. Last time I was here,
I was talking about my book.

00:03:27.206 --> 00:03:29.441
Thanks for having me again.
-Of course.

00:03:29.441 --> 00:03:30.843
-Yeah, I -- you know, people --

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:33.679
Every celebrity, like, gets
approached to do a podcast.

00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:35.815
And I was like, "I don't want to
be one of those people."

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:39.218
And I was working on the book,
and I just really wanted to

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:42.655
finish it so that there was
a POV and people could have

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:46.225
an understanding of, like, who I
am before I started the podcast.

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:48.094
But it's perfect for me.

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:50.897
I love writing essays,
and I love making TikToks.

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:52.732
And a podcast is right
in the middle.

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:54.033
-Yeah.
-So it's perfect.

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.935
I'm really, really enjoying it.

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:59.572
It's actually 2 episodes a week,
so it's 96 episodes a year.

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:01.006
It's a lot of work,
but I love it.

00:04:01.006 --> 00:04:02.275
-It's more work
than people think.

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:04.243
-It's so much work.
But I'm really enjoying it.

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:06.245
I'm actually getting a taste for
what you do a bit.

00:04:06.245 --> 00:04:08.247
-The interviewing side of it?
-The interviewing, yeah.

00:04:08.247 --> 00:04:10.115
One episode is an interview,
and one episode is

00:04:10.115 --> 00:04:13.052
a solo episode where I talk to
myself for 45 minutes.

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:15.454
-You have been interviewed
a great deal.

00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:18.390
Is there something that you've
learned being sort of a subject

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:20.392
of an interview, that you feel
like you are stealing

00:04:20.392 --> 00:04:22.128
to be an interviewer?

00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:25.064
-Yeah, I would say listening.
Sometimes interviewers don't

00:04:25.064 --> 00:04:27.433
listen, and they just kind of
stick to the, like,

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:29.669
"Oh, this is what we have to
hit, whatever."

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:31.671
And if you're listening,
you know, as somebody

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:33.806
in the audience, whatever,
it can be really frustrating

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:35.641
because you're like, "Wait.
They just said this thing,

00:04:35.641 --> 00:04:37.477
and I want you to pick up on
that and whatever."

00:04:37.477 --> 00:04:39.712
I feel like you do that so well.
You do listen. Howard Stern --

00:04:39.712 --> 00:04:41.214
-I'm sorry.
I drifted off a little.

00:04:41.214 --> 00:04:44.016
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:46.119
-That was good. That was good.
-No, I will say --

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:47.586
-Your kids don't think
you're funny?

00:04:47.586 --> 00:04:50.656
-I do -- Yeah, no kidding.

00:04:50.656 --> 00:04:52.792
Howard Stern, by the way,
is a perfect example.

00:04:52.792 --> 00:04:55.361
And I think it is true.

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:59.231
Whenever -- Because when I
started doing this show, I felt

00:04:59.231 --> 00:05:01.567
like there was no background
I had to actually be good.

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.970
But the one thing is being
a good listener, and it's true.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.205
And that's why people listen to
certain -- especially

00:05:06.205 --> 00:05:08.173
in the podcast form, where you
can't even see anything.

00:05:08.173 --> 00:05:10.142
You have to hear -- You kind of
have to picture it

00:05:10.142 --> 00:05:11.410
because the conversation is
connected.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:13.679
-Mm-hmm, yeah. No, it's harder
than what you do.

00:05:13.679 --> 00:05:15.447
Okay, Seth?
-No, no, no, it's not.

00:05:15.447 --> 00:05:17.383
I'm saying it's not because you
don't have the visual element.

00:05:17.383 --> 00:05:19.318
-Yeah. No, no, totally.

00:05:19.318 --> 00:05:21.154
But, yeah, it's really
enjoyable.

00:05:21.154 --> 00:05:22.655
I love talking to people.
I think of myself as

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:25.124
a curious person, and I'm just
really enjoying it.

00:05:25.124 --> 00:05:26.258
-You call it "High Low."
-Yeah.

00:05:26.258 --> 00:05:27.694
-Can you explain the title
real quick?

00:05:27.694 --> 00:05:31.163
-Yeah, so I have always been,
like, kind of fascinated by

00:05:31.163 --> 00:05:34.200
the idea of their being highbrow
or sophisticated topics

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.269
and lowbrow things,
and I think that I'm someone

00:05:37.269 --> 00:05:40.406
who, you know, people maybe
think of me in a certain way

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:42.408
in like an Instagram,
whatever, world,

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:43.642
and then I wrote
this book of essays.

00:05:43.642 --> 00:05:46.345
So I feel like I'm high and low
a bit.

00:05:46.345 --> 00:05:48.814
But I also just -- My favorite
way of talking about things is,

00:05:48.814 --> 00:05:51.617
like, marrying some, you know,
political idea with

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:53.152
like "Housewives" or whatever.

00:05:53.152 --> 00:05:56.155
So I don't really believe in
high and low essentially.

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:59.591
But it actually wasn't the first
choice for the name.

00:05:59.591 --> 00:06:01.427
-Oh. Because "High Low"
is a very good title.

00:06:01.427 --> 00:06:02.995
-Thank you.
-What was the first choice?

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:05.131
-Um, "Bitch Era."
-"Bitch Era"? Yeah.

00:06:05.131 --> 00:06:06.032
-Mm-hmm.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:07.933
Yeah, they flagged that one.

00:06:07.933 --> 00:06:10.168
Although I named
my media Company --

00:06:10.168 --> 00:06:12.104
-I did -- I actually --
When I was holding this up,

00:06:12.104 --> 00:06:14.373
I noticed that, yeah, Bitch Era
did make its way.

00:06:14.373 --> 00:06:16.042
-Yeah, yeah.
So that's my media company.

00:06:16.042 --> 00:06:17.976
I did make my way
to getting that.

00:06:17.976 --> 00:06:19.544
-That's good. It was available?

00:06:19.544 --> 00:06:21.047
-It was available.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:22.882
Hey, don't laugh.
-No, it's great.

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:24.583
-I think it's
a high in demand name.

00:06:24.583 --> 00:06:26.485
-Yeah, I can't believe --
I think a lot of people

00:06:26.485 --> 00:06:27.786
are kicking themselves
right now.

00:06:27.786 --> 00:06:28.987
They're like, "Why didn't I
think of 'Bitch Era'?"

00:06:28.987 --> 00:06:30.889
-Yeah, it's -- I personally feel
that way.

00:06:30.889 --> 00:06:33.392
I'm ready to make merch,
so if anybody else wants to be

00:06:33.392 --> 00:06:36.195
in their bitch era with me --
-In 2014, I went to NBC,

00:06:36.195 --> 00:06:37.463
I'm like,
"Bitch Era with Seth Meyers.

00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:38.698
Think about it." And they --

00:06:38.698 --> 00:06:40.599
[ Laughter ]
Same thing, they shut it down.

00:06:40.599 --> 00:06:41.767
Nobody has any vision
these days.

00:06:41.767 --> 00:06:43.569
-You're kind of
in your bitch era.

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:44.870
-I feel like I'm entering
my bitch era.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:46.772
-I like it. I see you in
the streets in New York --

00:06:46.772 --> 00:06:48.106
-Thank you.
I'm getting more bitchy.

00:06:48.106 --> 00:06:50.542
-Drinking those Negronis and --

00:06:50.542 --> 00:06:52.545
-Now, we should note, and this
is just something --

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:54.379
I guess -- Is that what you say
about a TikTok?

00:06:54.379 --> 00:06:56.181
Will a TikTok "drop"
later this evening?

00:06:56.181 --> 00:06:57.249
-Yeah, we made a TikTok.

00:06:57.249 --> 00:06:58.450
-We did.
We made a TikTok backstage.

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:00.119
-You hated it so much.
You hated the process.

00:07:00.119 --> 00:07:01.320
-I did.
-He did.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.222
[ Laughter ]

00:07:03.222 --> 00:07:05.190
-That's so weird because I'm
usually so good

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:06.392
at hiding how I feel.

00:07:06.392 --> 00:07:07.492
I did --
[ Laughter ]

00:07:07.492 --> 00:07:09.562
-Yeah.
-I did hate it so much.

00:07:09.562 --> 00:07:11.197
-You did a great job, though.
-Thank you.

00:07:11.197 --> 00:07:13.699
I mean, I will say, I was, like,
kind of hating it, and then I

00:07:13.699 --> 00:07:15.501
saw it, and I'm like,
"Oh, I'm really good at this."

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:17.537
-No, you were really good.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:17.537 --> 00:07:19.906
It was impressive because he
hadn't heard the sound before,

00:07:19.906 --> 00:07:21.474
but you love a Negroni.
-I love a Negroni.

00:07:21.474 --> 00:07:23.543
-But you didn't get to sip on
the Negroni.

00:07:23.543 --> 00:07:25.277
-Well, I couldn't sip on it
because we had --

00:07:25.277 --> 00:07:27.746
You know, I had a show.
And I take it very seriously.

00:07:27.746 --> 00:07:28.981
-Okay, that's great.

00:07:28.981 --> 00:07:31.683
-I have the one Negroni at 2:00
in the afternoon,

00:07:31.683 --> 00:07:33.452
and then I don't --

00:07:33.452 --> 00:07:35.788
-Are you considering, like,
using TikTok more?

00:07:35.788 --> 00:07:37.790
-Well, I mean, let's see how
it -- if it takes off.

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.525
-Is that part of your bitch era?
Is TikTok-ing?

00:07:40.525 --> 00:07:42.294
-That's how you'll know
my bitch era has started.

00:07:42.294 --> 00:07:44.029
If I become really active
on TikTok,

00:07:44.029 --> 00:07:45.264
then you'll be like,
"It's happened."

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:46.832
-I think everyone should be
on TikTok.

00:07:46.832 --> 00:07:48.333
It's amazing.
-You do love it, right?

00:07:48.333 --> 00:07:51.570
-Yeah, I love it, and the whole
thing about privacy, I'm like,

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:54.340
"Listen, Facebook, Instagram,
they've already stolen it.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.308
The Chinese just do better
things with it."

00:07:56.308 --> 00:07:57.910
-Yeah.
-They have our information.

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:00.379
-There's nothing about any of us

00:08:00.379 --> 00:08:01.847
that everybody doesn't already
know.

00:08:01.847 --> 00:08:04.149
-No, and I'm telling you
the algorithm, they really --

00:08:04.149 --> 00:08:05.517
they really know how to
entertain us.

00:08:05.517 --> 00:08:06.886
So I'm grateful for that.
-Okay.

00:08:06.886 --> 00:08:09.222
-I'm like, "Take my data and do
something great with it."

00:08:09.222 --> 00:08:10.256
-Okay. Well --
-Yeah.

00:08:10.256 --> 00:08:11.557
[ Laughter ]

00:08:11.557 --> 00:08:13.526
-It's really nice to hear
someone so optimistic about

00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:15.694
the fact that all our privacy
has been stolen.

00:08:15.694 --> 00:08:17.696
-I think it's like I've passed
the point of cynical.

00:08:17.696 --> 00:08:19.732
-You are past, yeah.
-And now I'm just like,

00:08:19.732 --> 00:08:22.034
"We have to just accept."
-Alright. Well, look,

00:08:22.034 --> 00:08:26.104
acceptance, bitch era, Seth on
TikTok, it's all happening.

00:08:26.104 --> 00:08:27.239
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:08:27.239 --> 00:08:27.906
-Thank you so much
for having me.

00:08:27.906 --> 00:08:29.108
-It's always a pleasure.

00:08:29.108 --> 00:08:31.110
New episodes of
"High Low with EmRata" available

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:33.178
Tuesdays and Thursdays wherever
you get your podcasts.

00:08:33.178 --> 00:08:35.047
We'll be right back with
Seth Reiss and Will Tracy.

